course: reflection
lesson: 20
title: Fights, racism and being a bystander
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some hurts are about losing face, like Rory losing an arm-wrestle to his little cousin at a barbecue while the whole family laughed; others cut much deeper, like a boy telling Mo every single day that he should "go back" to a country he has never lived in, when Mo was born right here.
explain: Mo turned it over every night: "Where's my country, then? Do I not belong anywhere?" For weeks he stopped talking about his family's food and language at school, as if hiding them would make the words stop.
explain: What changed it: he told his teacher, the school treated it seriously, the class did a project on where everyone's families come from, and his grandad's stories made him proud again; telling someone they don't belong because of their race or family is racist bullying, and it is never okay.
explain: Then there is Max, who just stood there while his friends bullied a younger kid: "I didn't do anything. But I didn't stop it either. Is that just as bad?" Doing nothing while someone is hurt can feel like being part of it; next time, getting help is the right thing.
explain: And fights between friends often start from a guess, like Ruby screaming "You did that on purpose!" at netball when it was an accident; checking first saves a lot of fights, and even screaming rows can be mended with a real sorry.
fact: Families often tease about funny moments; it usually means affection, but it can still sting, and laughing along over time turns it into a shared joke.
fact: Racism is treating people badly because of their race or where their family comes from; telling someone to "go back" where they supposedly came from is racist bullying and should always be reported.
fact: A person born in a country belongs there, and a family story from another place is something to be proud of, not hidden.
fact: Even friends who scream horrible things at each other can make up if both say sorry and mean it; "bury the hatchet" means stop fighting.
fact: Dyslexia is a difference that makes reading and spelling harder; it has nothing to do with being clever, and many very successful people have it.
fact: Sometimes we decide someone hurt us on purpose when it was an accident; "jumping to conclusions" causes many fights, and checking first prevents them.
fact: Peer pressure is when friends push you to do something you don't want to do; saying no is braver than giving in.
fact: When a teacher seems to pick on you, talking to a parent or another teacher can help sort it out; being rude back usually makes it worse.
fact: A bystander watches harm happen without helping; doing nothing can feel like being part of it, and telling a teacher afterwards still helps.
fact: Telling an adult when someone hurts you is not "grassing"; a grass, in UK slang, tells on people to get them in trouble, but telling to stay safe is different.
fact: Play-fighting can turn into real fighting; when someone says stop, the game must stop at once.
fact: Feelings after these events change over weeks: anger often turns to embarrassment, sadness or guilt, and then fades once things are put right.
exercise: Rory lost an arm-wrestle to his younger cousin and the family brings it up for months. What does the teasing usually mean? | Affection, though it can still sting | That the family thinks he is weak | That they want him to fight his cousin | That they are cross with him for losing
exercise: What helped Rory in the end? | Learning to laugh about it with them | Challenging his cousin again and losing | Refusing to go to family barbecues | Practising arm-wrestling every night
exercise: A boy tells Mo every day he should "go back" to another country, though Mo was born here. What is it? | Racist bullying, which is never okay | Normal joking that everyone has to put up with | A fair question about where Mo lives | Only rude if Mo was not born here
exercise: For weeks Mo hid his family's food and language at school. Did it help? | No; hiding his culture made him feel worse | Yes; hiding it made the bully stop at once | Yes; it is best to forget where your family is from | No, because he should have changed his name too
exercise: What helped Mo feel proud again? | Telling his teacher, a class project on families, his grandad's stories | Pretending to everyone that his family came from somewhere else entirely | Shouting the same thing back at the boy | Staying home until the boy moved away
exercise: Holly and her friend screamed horrible things at each other. Can they be friends again? | Yes, if both say sorry and mean it | No; one big fight ends any friendship | Only if they never talk about the fight | Only if one of them changes schools
exercise: Oscar is called stupid every day because of his dyslexia. What is true? | Dyslexia makes reading harder but has nothing to do with being clever | Dyslexia means a person can never learn to read at all | The bullies are right, slow reading means low intelligence | Dyslexia goes away if you just try harder at reading
exercise: Ruby screamed "You did that on purpose!" after being knocked over at netball. What went wrong? | She jumped to a conclusion; it may have been an accident | Her friend clearly meant to hurt her badly and had planned it all match | Netball is a game where pushing is the rule | She should have screamed even louder
exercise: Ben's mates pushed him to fight a boy to prove he isn't a coward. What is peer pressure? | Friends pushing you into something you don't want | Pressing your face against a window to peer out | Your teacher giving you too much homework | Two friends deciding something together fairly
exercise: What would have been braver for Ben? | Saying no to his mates | Fighting the boy twice | Hitting one of his mates | Running away from school
exercise: Lucy thinks her teacher hates her. What helped? | Her mum talking to the teacher | Being rude back to the teacher | Refusing to do any work at all | Asking to sit outside every lesson
exercise: Max watched his friends bully a younger kid and did nothing. What is the word for what he was? | A bystander | An upstander | A referee | A witness box
exercise: What can Max still do afterwards? | Tell a teacher and say sorry to the kid | Nothing; once it's over it can't be helped | Join the bullies so they stay his friends | Tell the younger kid to stop crying
exercise: Zoe was hit by a bully but tells nobody because she doesn't want to be "a grass". What is true? | Telling to stay safe isn't grassing | Telling on anyone is always grassing | She should hide her bruises instead | Grown-ups can't do anything anyway
exercise: Jacob's play-fight got rough and his mate didn't stop when Jacob said stop. What is the rule? | When someone says stop, the game stops | Play-fights only stop when someone wins | It's fine if both started it as fun | Saying stop is part of the game
test: Mo's mate from London says, "Bruv, you was born in Croydon, you're from here, innit." ("Bruv" means mate.) What does this show? | Mo belongs here, and the bully's words are racist and wrong | Mo should prove where he was born to the bully | Only people born in Croydon belong in Britain | Mo's mate secretly thinks the bully might have a point about where Mo is from
test: What would happen if the school ignored the "go back" comments? | The racist bullying would likely go on and Mo would keep hiding his culture | The bully would grow out of it within a week | Mo would stop caring and feel totally fine | It would make no difference to anyone
test: Holly thinks: "She said I'm fake. I said she's stupid. It's over forever." What actually worked? | A note saying "I miss you. Sorry." | Waiting forever for her friend to come back | Telling everyone her side of the fight | Making new friends to make her jealous
test: Oscar's classmate goes, "dyslexic kids are lowkey slow, no offence." What is the true reply? | Dyslexia is about reading, not cleverness; many brilliant people have it | He's right; dyslexia means you are slow at everything, not just at reading | It's fine to say because he said no offence | Dyslexia is just an excuse for not reading
test: Ruby's coach in Ireland says, "Ah, she just went off on her, the eejit." ("Went off on" means got very angry at; "eejit" means fool.) What should Ruby have done first? | Checked whether it was an accident | Screamed even louder to be heard | Knocked her friend over back so it was fair | Walked off the court for good
test: Ben's mates go, "don't be a wuss, just smack him, he's sus anyway." ("Sus" means suspicious, dodgy.) What does Ben wish he'd done? | Said no, because the boy never did anything to him | Hit him harder so the fight ended quicker | Asked his mates to go and fight the boy for him instead of doing it himself | Waited until after school to do it
test: Lucy thinks, "Whatever I do, I'm in trouble, she hates me." What would probably make it worse? | Being rude back to the teacher | Telling her mum how she feels | Asking the teacher what she can improve | Talking to another teacher she trusts
test: Max says, "It wasn't me, I just stood there." Why does he still feel guilty? | Doing nothing while someone is hurt can feel like being part of it | He secretly did hit the younger kid when nobody was looking at him | He is guilty about something else that happened | Standing still is against school rules
test: In a cartoon, the hero walks past a kid getting bullied because "it's not my business". The next episode he's still the hero. What's off? | Real heroes, even small ones, help or get help | Nothing; minding your own business is the most heroic thing to do | Heroes only help people they already know | Bullying is fine in cartoons, so it's not off
test: Zoe's gran from Manchester says, "Our kid, telling's not grassing when you're the one getting hurt." ("Our kid" is a family word for a younger relative.) Is she right? | Yes; telling to stay safe is not grassing | No; telling is always grassing | Only if the bully is older than her | Only if there are bruises
test: Jacob's mate says, "it was only a laugh, why you crying?" What's the real issue? | He didn't stop when Jacob said stop | Jacob can't take a joke at all | Play-fights must always end in tears | Jacob should have fought harder
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an instruction to a pupil to "go back" to another country on account of his family origin? | It constitutes racist bullying and ought to be reported | It constitutes a reasonable enquiry as to residence | It is permissible where the pupil was born abroad | It is a form of humour exempt from school regulations
exam: Identify the principal reason for which disputes arising from a collision during sport may be avoided. | Ascertaining whether the collision was accidental before reacting | Reacting with maximal anger before any facts are known | Declining to participate in any team sport whatsoever | Presuming in all cases that any collision was intentional and responding in kind
exam: It is given that a pupil witnessed the bullying of a younger child without intervening. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Subsequent disclosure to a teacher remains of value | Inaction confers complete freedom from any concern | The witness is obliged to join in the bullying | No action is possible once the incident has concluded
exam: In respect of dyslexia, determine which statement is correct. | It renders reading and spelling more difficult and is unrelated to intelligence | It indicates a general deficiency in intelligence | It is eliminated entirely by increased effort | It affects only persons over the age of eighteen
